Overview|Overview|A summary of the opened file's declared architecture, format, layout and metadata.|Start here to answer whether a file targets x86 or ARM64, where its entry point lies, and whether structural warnings need attention.|This is static inspection: opening a file does not run it. Summary values reflect headers, not a guarantee that Windows can load it.
Headers|Headers|Headers describe how to interpret and load an image. DOS means legacy Disk Operating System; COFF means Common Object File Format. The historically named optional header is required for PE images.|Inspect named fields with their offsets and widths to connect the specification to actual bytes.|Editing changes declarations, not the code or data they describe. Related fields may need coordinated changes.
Sections|Sections|Sections divide the image into ranges of code or data with file locations, memory locations and permission flags.|Compare .text and .data to see why executable code and writable data have different permissions and sizes.|The editor can rename section labels and edit existing header values. It does not rebuild sections, move their contents or update references automatically.
Directories|Directories|Data directories are address-and-size pairs that locate important structures such as imports, exports and resources.|Use a directory to locate a structure even when its section has an unfamiliar name. Its help explains whether the app decodes that structure.|Most addresses are RVAs; the certificate entry uses a file offset. Changing a descriptor does not create or rebuild its payload.
