FindNext|Find next|Searches onward for the next occurrence of the entered byte sequence.|Use it to inspect repeated signatures or text encoded as known bytes. Several matches may exist in unrelated structures.|A match establishes only equal bytes, not their meaning or runtime use. Searching does not modify the document; check the location before any replacement.
Replace|Replace bytes|Overwrites a selected file range with a byte sequence of the same length.|Use a tiny, understood change in a separate copy, then inspect its before/after bytes and try Undo.|This is not insertion or deletion. Preserving length avoids shifting the file, but arbitrary replacements can still damage instructions, addresses, tables or signatures.
Previous|Previous page|Moves the hex view and cursor selection to the preceding page of file bytes.|Use page navigation to inspect data around a range without entering another offset. Check the cursor and page indicators after moving.|This changes navigation only, not file contents. Crossing a display-page boundary does not imply a PE section or structure boundary.
Next|Next page|Moves the hex view and cursor selection to the following page of file bytes.|Use it to continue examining a long section or pattern. Follow the offset labels and cursor indicator to understand the selected file position.|This changes navigation only. File pages here are a viewing convenience, not necessarily the operating system's memory pages.
PE|Portable Executable (PE)|PE is the Windows executable image format used by many EXE, DLL and system files. Headers describe how bytes should be mapped into memory.|Use PE Workshop to learn this layout, inspect declared dependencies and metadata, and make controlled patches to a copy.|It is not a source-code editor, malware verdict or general feature editor. The application reads structures without running the image.
Extensions|File extensions|EXE means executable; DLL means dynamic-link library; SYS commonly identifies a system driver; OCX identifies an ActiveX control; EFI identifies an Extensible Firmware Interface image.|These suffixes suggest intended use. Actual bytes determine whether the file contains a supported PE image; check Format and Machine for structural details.|Renaming a suffix does not convert the format, code or behavior. The parser still requires valid PE headers and supported bounds.
FixedSize|Fixed-size editing|Edits replace bytes while retaining the file's length and existing storage layout.|This is useful for learning individual fields and making narrow, deliberate patches whose location you can review in Changes.|The app does not insert new sections, grow tables or rebuild resources. Equal-length edits can still invalidate references, loading behavior or a signature.
Architecture|Architecture|The COFF Machine value declares the target CPU family, such as x86, x64 or ARM64.|Use this to distinguish a CPU mismatch from an ordinary dependency problem. PE32+ identifies a header format, not a particular CPU family by itself.|Changing Machine does not translate instructions or convert an executable to another architecture. Unknown values require checking the producer and specification.
Format|PE32 / PE32+ format|The optional-header Magic selects PE32 or PE32+, which use different layouts and widths for several values.|Use this when interpreting image base, stack and heap fields. PE32+ uses wider address-related fields but still has 32-bit RVAs.|Changing Magic alone does not convert layouts or code. PE32+ is not synonymous with x64; check Machine for the declared CPU.
FileSize|File size|The number of stored disk bytes, including headers and section data. KiB means kibibyte, or 1,024 bytes; MiB means mebibyte, or 1,048,576 bytes.|Compare it with Image size to see why mapped memory size can differ from disk length.|Fixed-size edits keep file length constant. File size is neither a count of useful code nor the total memory a running process uses.
ImageStructure|Image structure|The overview bar compares sections using their declared raw byte sizes, with a legend linking the section names to explanations.|Use it to see which sections account for most stored section data, then inspect exact file and memory ranges in Sections.|The bar is a size comparison, not a to-scale map of all disk offsets or memory addresses. Section names are conventions; diagnostics remain necessary for structural interpretation.
ImageMetadata|Image metadata|Metadata includes producer-supplied labels and values such as timestamps, checksum and subsystem.|Use it for comparisons between builds and to learn what a linker records. Treat each value according to its own format and purpose.|Metadata can be edited or generated deterministically. It is not proof of provenance, creation time, safety or the program's actual operating-system compatibility.
FileDetails|File details|The overview groups header-location and metadata values such as PE offset, header size, checksum and COFF timestamp.|Use their individual help to connect structural locations with bytes and distinguish a checksum from signing or a timestamp from creation-time proof.|These values are declarations and metadata, not a trust verdict. Editing them does not automatically repair other structures or verify that a saved copy will run.
EntryPoint|Entry point|An RVA identifying the image's initial execution location, or zero when no entry point is declared.|In Hex, select RVA and Go to this value to inspect its mapped startup bytes. The target often contains runtime initialization rather than the source function main.|Revealing the header field shows the value's storage, not target code. Changing it can skip required setup; some targets have no stored bytes and the app does not disassemble instructions.
ImageBase|Image base|The preferred virtual address where the loader would place the image in a process.|Compare it with an RVA: a virtual address is the actual load base plus the RVA. Address randomization can choose another actual base.|Editing this value alone is not complete rebasing. Absolute references and relocation records must remain consistent with the placement.
ImageSize|Image size|SizeOfImage declares the memory span of the loaded image, including headers and aligned sections.|Compare it with file size and section virtual ranges to understand stored bytes versus mapped memory.|It must remain consistent with section ranges and SectionAlignment. Enlarging this number does not create new section data or change the process's total memory use.
Subsystem|Subsystem|Declares the environment expected by the image, for example Windows GUI, Windows console or an EFI environment.|Use this to understand why a typical GUI executable and a console executable have different header values.|Changing this declaration does not add a console interface or adapt code to another environment. Existing startup code and platform assumptions still matter.
PeOffset|PE header offset|The DOS header's e_lfanew value gives the file offset of the PE signature and following headers.|Use it to jump from the initial MZ header to the PE structure rather than assuming a fixed location.|It is a disk offset, not an RVA. Changing it makes the parser and loader look elsewhere; it does not move the actual headers.
HeaderSize|Header size|SizeOfHeaders declares the file range occupied by the DOS, PE, optional and section headers, rounded for file alignment.|Compare it with the section table and first raw section offset to see where structured headers end.|It must contain the real header tables and meet alignment requirements. Changing it does not move tables or insert padding bytes.
Checksum|PE checksum|A non-cryptographic check value stored in the optional header and calculated from file bytes.|Compare the stored value after an edit or use Update checksum to recompute it. Certain image types are subject to loader checksum checks.|This is not a signature or safety verdict. Recomputing it does not restore trust or repair an Authenticode signature invalidated by modified content.
Timestamp|COFF timestamp|A 32-bit producer-supplied value commonly interpreted as seconds since 1970, with special cases and reproducible-build uses.|Use it as one comparison clue between images, alongside other metadata. Zero and 0xFFFFFFFF are not meaningful date/time stamps.|It is not proof of the actual creation time. Changing it only changes recorded bytes and may invalidate signing; it does not rebuild the program.
Diagnostics|Diagnostics|Messages identify structural issues or limits encountered while parsing headers and supported tables.|Use them to locate misaligned ranges, invalid mappings or truncated auxiliary information before trusting a displayed table's completeness.|Warnings are not a malware verdict or complete loader validation. An absence of warnings does not prove that the image will run or is safe.
