RawOffset|Raw file offset|The file position of a section's stored data, represented by PointerToRawData.|Use it to reveal the section's first byte in Hex. This coordinate is measured from the disk file's beginning.|It differs from the section's RVA. Changing the pointer does not move data and can make the loader interpret a different range or an invalid one.
RawSize|Raw size|The number of bytes assigned to a section's stored data, normally rounded to FileAlignment.|Compare it with Virtual size to understand alignment padding and data that will be zero-filled in memory.|Changing this count does not grow or shrink the file. The range must still fit within the file and remain consistent with alignment and other sections.
Permissions|Section permissions|R, W and X summarize whether section flags request readable, writable and executable memory.|Compare .text with .data to understand how code and mutable state are separated. A dash means that permission bit is not set.|These letters show requested section attributes, not proof of runtime access or safety. Changing flags can make existing code fail or expose writable executable memory.
SectionFlags|Section characteristics|A 32-bit mask describing one section's contents and attributes, including read, write and execute requests.|Use the PE section-flags definitions to interpret combined bits; the permissions column shows only the R/W/X subset.|These bits differ from the COFF header's 16-bit Characteristics mask. Editing permissions does not rewrite contents or ensure runtime compatibility.
DirectoryIndex|Directory index|The slot number, from zero to fifteen, which defines a data directory's standardized meaning.|Use index 0 for exports, 1 for imports and 4 for certificates when connecting a specification to displayed rows.|The index is structural, not an editable sort order. Reordering entries would change their interpretation even if their addresses remained unchanged.
DirectoryName|Directory name|A friendly label for a standardized data-directory slot, such as Import or Resource.|Read its help to see what that structure represents and whether the app decodes its contents or only displays its location.|The name comes from the slot's defined meaning, not an embedded string you can rename. A directory can be absent with zero address and size.
DirectoryAddress|Directory address (RVA)|For ordinary directories, this value is the RVA of the structure's beginning in the mapped image.|Use Reveal to follow the declared range when it maps to stored file bytes. The certificate directory is the special file-offset exception.|Changing the address redirects readers to existing bytes. It does not move, create or rebuild the table, and an unmapped address may make it unreadable.
CertificateAddress|Certificate file offset|The certificate directory's address identifies a byte position in the disk file, rather than an RVA in loaded memory.|Use this offset with its size to locate the declared embedded certificate range. Certificates are not loaded like ordinary image sections.|Changing this pointer does not sign the image or verify certificate data. It must identify a valid file range; signing requires a separate signing workflow.
DirectorySize|Directory size|The declared extent, in bytes, of the structure associated with this data-directory entry.|Use it with the address to check the bounds of a table and understand why the app may report an invalid range.|Changing the size does not add, remove or rewrite table records. The descriptor must agree with existing bytes and the format's structure-specific rules.
AddressType|Address type|Identifies the coordinate system of a value: normally RVA for a directory, but file offset for certificates.|In Hex, select File offset or RVA to match the number you are navigating to. Certificate addresses use the File offset mode.|These coordinate systems are not interchangeable. A memory range may have no raw backing, and the app can only reveal addresses that map to stored bytes.
Module|Import module|The dependency module name recorded in an ordinary import descriptor, commonly a DLL name.|Use it to identify declared dependencies or compare imports between builds. The runtime may apply search rules, API-set resolution and other loading behavior.|The app does not locate or load this DLL. The name alone does not prove which physical file will be used or that the dependency is trustworthy.
ImportSymbol|Imported symbol|A function or data name requested from a module; an ordinal-only import is shown with a numeric marker.|Use it to investigate a missing imported symbol or understand a binary's declared interface with its dependencies.|This is a declaration, not evidence that execution calls the function. Dynamic and delay-loaded dependencies may not appear in this ordinary-import table.
ExportSymbol|Exported symbol|A name or ordinal identifying an interface exposed by this image to other modules.|Use it to compare DLL interfaces or locate a specific exported entry. Multiple names can refer to the same address.|An export can be data, code or a forwarder. The app does not recover source types, argument lists or implementation behavior from the name.
Hint|Import hint|A small index hint that can help the loader find an imported name in the target module's export name table.|Use it when comparing raw import records with a specification. It is an optimization hint, not the function's address or public ordinal.|Ordinal-only imports do not use a hint/name record, so the UI shows a dash for their Hint. That dash means not applicable, rather than a missing function address.
IAT|Import address table (IAT) RVA|The relative address of the slot associated with this imported symbol, later used for its resolved address.|Use it to locate the import slot in the file or compare it with the IAT directory's range.|This app inspects file bytes, not a running process. It does not resolve DLLs or show the final function addresses written by the loader.
Ordinal|Ordinal|A numeric identifier used to import or export a symbol without requiring its textual name.|Use it when a library exports entries only by number, or compare an ordinal with a client's declared import.|Ordinals are module-specific identifiers, not file offsets or global function numbers. Changing them can break clients that depend on that particular binary interface.
Forwarder|Export forwarder|An export entry that names another module's symbol rather than pointing directly to this image's implementation.|For example, MODULE.Function or MODULE.#123 redirects symbol resolution elsewhere. Use this to understand an export whose RVA points inside the export directory.|The app displays the string but does not load the destination module or verify resolution. This entry is not ordinary executable code to patch.
Description|Description|A human-readable explanation of a field or edit that supplements its name, location and value.|Use it to understand a change record or relate an abbreviated header name to its structural purpose.|Descriptions are UI text, not evidence about the file's producer or behavior. Detailed field help remains necessary when editing a pointer, size or flag mask.
Before|Before bytes|The original bytes at the recorded patch location before that edit was applied.|Compare them with After to see exactly what changed, including little-endian order for numeric fields.|They describe a particular session edit, not necessarily the original bytes of every file previously opened. Undo uses the recorded prior data for reversible experiments.
After|After bytes|The replacement bytes written at the recorded location by a session edit.|Compare them with Before and inspect the affected field or hex range before saving a copy.|Equal-length patches preserve disk positions but can still change parsing, runtime behavior or signing validity. The history describes the edit, not its correctness.
GoTo|Go to address|The input chooses a location for the hex cursor using the selected File offset or RVA coordinate system.|In File offset mode, enter 0x3C to inspect the DOS e_lfanew storage. In RVA mode, enter the entry-point value to follow its mapped bytes.|An out-of-range offset or memory-only RVA cannot be inspected. Navigation leaves the document unchanged; decimal and 0x-prefixed hexadecimal numbers are accepted.
Search|Search byte pattern or ASCII text|Finds a literal sequence in the file using Hex bytes or ASCII text mode, without interpreting instructions or PE structures.|Search for 4D 5A in Hex bytes mode or a simple DLL name in ASCII text mode. Find next continues onward and wraps to the start.|ASCII search supports characters 0–127. Several matches may have unrelated meanings; equal bytes do not establish runtime use or a safe replacement target.
HexBytes|Hexadecimal bytes|Each pair of hexadecimal digits represents one byte, from 00 through FF. Hex uses digits 0–9 and A–F.|Use this view for exact comparisons with signatures, field storage and patches. For example, 4D 5A is the initial MZ signature.|Numeric PE fields use little-endian byte order. Editing bytes bypasses the meaning of structures, so confirm the range and interpretation first.
ASCII|ASCII text|ASCII means American Standard Code for Information Interchange. It assigns byte values to characters in the range 0–127. The hex view renders printable characters and substitutes placeholders otherwise.|Choose ASCII text search to find a simple name without converting it to byte pairs. Confirm matches using offsets and hex values.|Binary bytes can coincidentally look like text. ASCII search does not decode Unicode strings or determine runtime use.
DecodedText|Decoded text|A text rendering interprets displayed bytes using the view's supported encoding and substitutes or hides unprintable data.|Use it as a reading aid for recognizable strings while keeping byte offsets and hex values as the exact reference.|This is not source recovery, a resource decoder or proof that a program displays the text. Multibyte strings may cross view boundaries.
HexColumns|Hex columns|The column position identifies a byte's displacement within a displayed row; the row address is its starting file offset.|Add the column position to the row offset to locate a byte precisely. This helps compare hex data with an offset listed in Headers.|Rows are a display arrangement, not PE records or instruction boundaries. Selecting a column does not establish the width of a field.
Cursor|Hex cursor|The current selected byte location in the file view, reported as a file offset.|Use it to anchor a replacement or keep track of a revealed field while inspecting neighboring bytes.|A cursor is a viewing selection, not a loaded instruction pointer. Any replacement affects bytes at the chosen disk position, so verify its range.
Page|Hex page|A bounded portion of the file shown at one time to keep inspection responsive and readable.|Use Previous, Next or Go to reach another range while following the page indicator and file offsets.|These are editor display pages, not PE sections or necessarily operating-system memory pages. Changing pages does not modify the file.
ReplacementBytes|Replacement bytes|The exact hexadecimal byte sequence proposed for overwriting an existing range.|Separate byte pairs as the input requires, then compare the proposed count and location before applying. A small patch is easier to review and undo.|The replacement must fit the fixed-size operation. It does not insert text or rebuild structures, and can invalidate a signature even when the file still parses.
NumericInput|Numeric input|A field editor accepts an unsigned numeric value, commonly decimal or hexadecimal prefixed with 0x.|Use decimal for counts and 0x notation for addresses or flag masks. Read the field help to check its units and width.|The value must fit the field's original bytes. Acceptance of a number does not guarantee a valid alignment, mapping, flag combination or runtime behavior.
SectionNames|Section-name input|A section label occupies eight bytes and is interpreted here as ASCII text, with unused bytes padded.|Use a short name such as .learn for a reversible experiment on a copy, then observe the changed section header.|Keep the name within eight ASCII bytes. Renaming a section does not relocate its contents, change permissions, add a feature or rewrite directory references.
""";

    private const string Fields = """
Field.e_lfanew|e_lfanew|This DOS-header field points to the PE signature using a disk file offset.|Reveal shows the four stored bytes; Go to the value to inspect the actual PE header. This connects the initial MZ stub to the Windows image layout.|Changing the pointer does not move headers. An incorrect value can make the entire file fail PE parsing.
Field.Machine|Machine|The COFF target-machine code identifies the CPU family expected by the image.|Compare it with the friendly Architecture label to distinguish x86, x64, ARM64 and hybrid ARM variants. PE32+ alone does not identify the CPU.|Changing this code does not translate machine instructions, calling conventions or runtime dependencies; it cannot convert an architecture.
Field.NumberOfSections|NumberOfSections|The count tells readers how many forty-byte section headers follow the optional header.|Use it to relate the header declaration to the Sections view. PE Workshop supports the Windows limit of up to 96 sections.|Changing the count does not insert or delete section records. A larger count can interpret unrelated bytes as headers or exceed file bounds.
Field.TimeDateStamp|TimeDateStamp|A COFF producer value commonly interpreted as seconds since 1970, with special values and reproducible-build exceptions.|Compare builds using this as one clue rather than proof of creation time. Zero and 0xFFFFFFFF do not represent meaningful timestamps.|A small change is useful for a reversible metadata experiment, but it changes signed content. It does not rebuild code or alter the real creation history.
Field.PointerToSymbolTable|PointerToSymbolTable|A disk offset to a COFF symbol table, or zero when none is declared. This older debugging mechanism is deprecated for executable images.|Use it to distinguish an old COFF symbol reference from modern PDB debug records. The app displays the field but does not decode that symbol table.|Changing it does not generate symbols or recover source names. For images this field should normally be zero.
Field.NumberOfSymbols|NumberOfSymbols|The number of records in the COFF symbol table referenced by PointerToSymbolTable.|Use it with that pointer when investigating legacy structures. It is not a count of imported functions, exports or source-code functions.|This older debugging mechanism is deprecated for images, where the value should normally be zero. Editing the count does not create symbol records.
Field.SizeOfOptionalHeader|SizeOfOptionalHeader|The byte length of the header following COFF, whose name is historical: it is required for these executable images.|Use it to locate the section table and verify that the declared directory records fit within the optional header.|Changing the length shifts where readers expect the section table without moving any bytes. Magic and directory layout must remain consistent with it.
Field.Characteristics|COFF Characteristics|A sixteen-bit mask describing attributes of the overall image, for example executable-image or DLL status.|Use COFF flag definitions to interpret the combined value. These flags are different from a section's thirty-two-bit memory and content attributes.|Setting a DLL flag does not create exports or DLL initialization code. Changing image attributes can conflict with existing startup and loader assumptions.
Field.Magic|Optional-header Magic|The value 0x10B selects PE32; 0x20B selects PE32+. This determines how the optional header is laid out.|Use it to explain why ImageBase and stack/heap sizes have different widths, and why BaseOfData exists only in PE32.|Changing Magic alone does not convert the layout or program. The declared CPU still comes from Machine, not this format marker.
Field.MajorLinkerVersion|MajorLinkerVersion|The major part of the linker version recorded by the image producer.|Compare it with MinorLinkerVersion when investigating build metadata. It may give a toolchain clue, but producers can supply or modify it.|Changing this byte does not rebuild the file with another linker. It is metadata rather than a guarantee about provenance, compatibility or quality.
Field.MinorLinkerVersion|MinorLinkerVersion|The minor part of the producer's recorded linker version, paired with MajorLinkerVersion.|Use the pair as a comparison clue between builds or toolchains, while checking other records for stronger evidence.|Changing it alters recorded metadata only. It does not change machine code or establish which linker actually generated the file.
Field.SizeOfCode|SizeOfCode|The declared total size of code sections, as recorded in the optional header.|Compare it with section raw sizes and code-related flags to understand how the producer summarizes code storage.|It is not a count of instructions or functions. Editing it does not add code, change section contents or disassemble the program.
Field.SizeOfInitializedData|SizeOfInitializedData|The declared aggregate size of initialized data sections, which carry initial bytes in the file.|Compare it with .data and other data-bearing sections to understand the producer's storage summary.|Changing this number does not resize those sections or change their variables. It is not the total memory used by a process while running.
Field.SizeOfUninitializedData|SizeOfUninitializedData|The declared aggregate size of uninitialized data sections, whose memory can be zero-filled rather than fully stored on disk.|Use it with virtual and raw section sizes to see why a memory image can be larger than its file-backed contents.|Changing it does not allocate new variables or rebuild sections. Actual section layout and runtime allocations determine memory use.
Field.AddressOfEntryPoint|AddressOfEntryPoint|The RVA at which initial execution is intended to begin; zero can indicate no entry point.|In Hex, select RVA and Go to this value to inspect mapped startup bytes. Field Reveal shows the value's storage in the header, not target instructions.|Startup is often runtime initialization rather than main. Redirecting it requires valid code and correct setup; changing the value cannot safely select any arbitrary function.
Field.BaseOfCode|BaseOfCode|The RVA marking the beginning of the code area as summarized by the producer.|Compare it with executable sections to connect the optional header to the image layout. It is relative to the image base, not a file pointer.|Editing it does not move code or fix references. The app displays bytes and mappings but does not analyze instructions to validate the declaration.
Field.BaseOfData|BaseOfData|A PE32-only field giving the RVA of the beginning of the data area.|Compare it with writable or initialized-data sections to learn the PE32 layout. PE32+ omits this field rather than giving it a wider version.|Changing it does not move data or update instructions that reference data. It is not interchangeable with PointerToRawData.
Field.ImageBase|ImageBase|The preferred virtual load address, stored in four bytes for PE32 and eight bytes for PE32+.|Use base plus RVA to understand virtual addresses. The actual load address can differ because of relocation or address randomization.|Changing one header field is not complete rebasing: absolute references, relocations and loader requirements must remain consistent.
Field.SectionAlignment|SectionAlignment|The alignment unit, in bytes, used for section placement in the loaded image.|Compare section RVAs and SizeOfImage against it. A typical desktop image aligns mapped sections more broadly than file data.|It must meet PE alignment rules and match the existing layout. Changing it does not repack sections or update their addresses.
Field.FileAlignment|FileAlignment|The alignment unit, in bytes, used for section raw offsets and sizes in the disk file.|Use it to explain padding between stored ranges and why Raw size can exceed useful content.|It must be a valid power of two and consistent with SectionAlignment and the existing ranges. Changing it does not add padding or reposition bytes.
Field.MajorOperatingSystemVersion|MajorOperatingSystemVersion|The major part of the operating-system version recorded in the optional header.|Read it with the minor part when comparing producer metadata. It is not sufficient evidence that the application's APIs work on every version at or above it.|Changing the number does not add support for another OS. Real compatibility depends on code, dependencies and runtime behavior.
Field.MinorOperatingSystemVersion|MinorOperatingSystemVersion|The minor part of the optional header's recorded operating-system version.|Use it alongside the major value to compare declarations between builds. These values do not by themselves prove minimum requirements or tested compatibility.|Editing it changes a header declaration only. It cannot supply missing platform APIs or adapt an application's implementation.
Field.MajorImageVersion|MajorImageVersion|The major part of an image version recorded by the producer in the optional header.|Use it as a build-comparison clue, paired with MinorImageVersion. It is separate from a version-information resource or package version.|Changing it does not edit the resource tree or replace program features. A producer may leave these values at zero or use them differently.
Field.MinorImageVersion|MinorImageVersion|The minor part of the producer-recorded image version, paired with MajorImageVersion.|Compare it between builds while remembering that file version resources may carry different values and are not decoded here.|Editing this field changes only optional-header metadata. It does not update installers, resources or actual program implementation.
Field.MajorSubsystemVersion|MajorSubsystemVersion|The major part of the version recorded for the declared subsystem environment.|Use it with Subsystem and the minor version to interpret loader-related declarations; do not treat it as proof of tested platform support.|Changing a version declaration cannot add missing APIs or make code compatible with a different environment. Loader behavior and application requirements still matter.
Field.MinorSubsystemVersion|MinorSubsystemVersion|The minor part of the version associated with the declared subsystem.|Compare it with the major value when investigating build or loader differences. This field alone does not establish the program's full operating-system requirements.|Editing it may affect how a loader interprets the image but does not adapt program code or verify compatibility.
Field.Win32VersionValue|Win32VersionValue|A reserved optional-header field that should be zero, despite its version-like name.|Use its presence to learn that not every named numeric field is meaningful user metadata. A nonzero value deserves investigation of the producer.|Keep it zero for specification-conforming images. It is not a supported place to write a Windows version or add application behavior.
Field.SizeOfImage|SizeOfImage|The declared total mapped image span, including headers and sections rounded for SectionAlignment.|Compare it with the highest section range and File size to distinguish memory layout from disk storage.|Changing it does not create data or resize sections. An inconsistent or misaligned span can cause structural diagnostics or loader failure.
Field.SizeOfHeaders|SizeOfHeaders|The combined DOS, PE and section-header size, rounded to FileAlignment.|Use it to locate the declared header boundary and compare it with the first raw section range.|It must contain all header records and fit the file. Changing it does not move the section table or repair overlapping ranges.
Field.CheckSum|CheckSum|The stored PE checksum is a non-cryptographic check value calculated over the file.|Use Update checksum after an experiment when you want the saved declaration to match current bytes. Certain image types receive loader checksum checks.|Manually entering a value does not verify it. The checksum is not Authenticode signing and cannot restore a signature invalidated by changed content.
Field.Subsystem|Subsystem|A numeric code identifying the execution environment, such as Windows GUI or Windows console.|Compare GUI and console samples to see how the header expresses their expected environment. It also has values for specialized environments such as EFI.|Changing it does not redesign startup code, add a console workflow or port the program to another platform.
Field.DllCharacteristics|DllCharacteristics|A sixteen-bit optional-header flag mask describing loader and security-related image attributes, including dynamic-base and NX compatibility.|Use the specification to interpret bits and compare builds' declarations. Despite the name, this field also applies to EXE images.|Changing flags does not create the supporting relocation, guard or other tables, nor prove a mitigation works. It is distinct from the COFF DLL flag.
Field.SizeOfStackReserve|SizeOfStackReserve|The declared virtual address space to reserve for the initial stack, using the format's four- or eight-byte width.|Compare it with Stack commit to distinguish reserved space from memory initially committed for use.|Increasing a reserve does not rewrite stack-heavy code or guarantee it cannot overflow. Keep reserve, commit, alignment and platform constraints consistent.
Field.SizeOfStackCommit|SizeOfStackCommit|The declared amount of stack memory initially committed, which can grow within the reserved range.|Use it with Stack reserve to understand initial availability versus maximum reserved address space.|Changing it affects a loader declaration rather than the number of local variables or recursive calls. It must remain sensible relative to the reserve and platform rules.
Field.SizeOfHeapReserve|SizeOfHeapReserve|The declared address-space reserve for the initial process heap, distinct from memory immediately committed.|Compare it with Heap commit when learning how header defaults influence initial allocation setup.|It is not a cap on all allocations made by the program or additional heaps. Editing the number does not solve memory leaks or rewrite allocation behavior.
Field.SizeOfHeapCommit|SizeOfHeapCommit|The declared amount of memory initially committed for the process heap.|Use it with Heap reserve to distinguish initial committed storage from reserved address space and later runtime growth.|Changing this metadata does not determine every heap allocation. Keep it consistent with the reserve and platform constraints; it cannot repair incorrect memory management.
Field.LoaderFlags|LoaderFlags|A reserved optional-header field that should be zero for these images.|Use it as a reminder that some legacy structure members have no supported custom interpretation in modern PE images.|Keep it zero. The app can display its storage but assigning arbitrary bits does not enable documented loader features or implement an application option.
Field.NumberOfRvaAndSizes|NumberOfRvaAndSizes|The number of data-directory address/size pairs declared at the end of the optional header.|Compare it with the Directories view and SizeOfOptionalHeader. PE Workshop displays the first sixteen supported standard entries.|Increasing it does not allocate directory storage or create payloads. The declared records must physically fit inside the existing optional header.
SectionField.VirtualSize|Section VirtualSize|The section's declared size in mapped memory, before final SectionAlignment rounding.|Compare it with SizeOfRawData to recognize file padding or a tail that is zero-filled in memory.|Changing the declaration does not add disk bytes or move other sections. Keep its virtual range consistent with neighboring sections and SizeOfImage.
SectionField.VirtualAddress|Section VirtualAddress|The RVA where this section begins after the image is loaded into memory.|Use it with Raw offset to map an address inside the section to file bytes: raw offset plus the displacement from this RVA.|It is not an absolute virtual address or file pointer. Editing it does not update code references, directories or relocation data that depend on the old location.
SectionField.SizeOfRawData|Section SizeOfRawData|The byte count of this section's stored file range, normally rounded to FileAlignment.|Compare it with VirtualSize to distinguish stored padding from useful mapped data. Uninitialized sections may store little or no raw data.|Changing it does not resize the file. The declared range must fit disk bounds, respect alignment and avoid unintended overlap with other sections.
SectionField.PointerToRawData|Section PointerToRawData|The disk file offset where this section's raw bytes begin, paired with its memory-relative VirtualAddress.|Revealing this field shows the pointer's header storage. Reveal the section row, or use Hex in File offset mode with this value, to inspect its payload.|Changing it redirects readers to existing bytes without moving content. Zero-sized sections may have no meaningful raw range to reveal.
SectionField.PointerToRelocations|Section PointerToRelocations|A file offset to COFF relocation records associated with the section, mainly meaningful in object-file workflows.|Distinguish this legacy section member from the image's Base relocation directory used when loading at another base.|For executable images it should be zero. Editing this pointer does not create base relocations or rebase the image; the app does not decode COFF relocation tables.
SectionField.PointerToLinenumbers|Section PointerToLinenumbers|A file offset to old COFF line-number records, a deprecated debugging mechanism for executable images.|Use it to distinguish historical line records from modern PDB debug information and from source text.|For these images it should be zero. Changing it cannot recover source lines or generate valid debug records, and the app does not decode this table.
SectionField.NumberOfRelocations|Section NumberOfRelocations|The count of the section's COFF relocation records, mainly used for object files before linking.|Use it with PointerToRelocations to interpret the old section-header layout, rather than confusing it with the base relocation directory.|For executable images it should be zero. Changing the count does not create records or adjust absolute references when changing ImageBase.
SectionField.NumberOfLinenumbers|Section NumberOfLinenumbers|The count of legacy COFF line-number entries referenced by this section header.|Use it when comparing historical structure definitions; it is not the count of source-code lines or modern debugging symbols.|This debugging mechanism is deprecated and the value should be zero for executable images. Editing it cannot recover source or build line records.
SectionField.Characteristics|Section Characteristics|A thirty-two-bit mask describing this section's code/data attributes and requested memory permissions.|Use its read, write and execute bits to explain the R/W/X summary, and the PE section-flags definitions for remaining bits.|This mask differs from COFF Characteristics. Changing permissions does not rewrite contents or guarantee the section still meets code and loader assumptions.
